  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PHP_FUNCTIONS
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PHP_FUNCTIONS
  language: 2
  conceptTitle: Functions
  symbol: f( )
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PHP_08_FUNCTIONS
  - HZ_PHP_06_FUNCTIONS
  topics:
  - Functions
  definition: A function is a named, reusable block of code that does one job. You
    write it once and call it whenever you need it.
  howItWorks: Create one with the keyword function, a name, round brackets with any
    parameters (inputs) and curly braces around the code. return sends a value back.
    When you call a function, give it one value (an argument) for each parameter.
  exampleCode: "<?php\nfunction addBonus($points) {\n    return $points + 5;\n}\necho
    addBonus(10);\n?>"
  exampleExplanation: addBonus(10) calls the function and gives the parameter $points
    the value 10. The function returns 10 + 5, so echo outputs 15.
  tip: A parameter is the name in the definition ($points). An argument is the value
    you pass in the call (10).
